\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces An example, in task space, of one model across learning, showing samples from: the initialization of the policy distribution (orange), the prior (blue), and the final policy distribution (green). All samples are decoded from action space to task space using the decoder. The trajectory over learning of the mean of the policy is shown in red. The goal state is shown as a red star, and the starting state as a green star. This subject's initial policy is a spherical Gaussian, and learning is successful in ``finding'' the target through accumulation of reward.\relax }}{182}{figure.caption.85}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Trajectories of the policy means across learning iterations for all seven subjects modeled. All starting policies are spherical Gaussians, and all subjects learn to reach the target. Trajectories are shown for two levels of the KL divergence weighting, $\alpha =0.0$ and $\alpha =0.5$, as explained in the main text.\relax }}{183}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces The trajectories of policy means shown in task space over learning from the start state (green star) to the goal state (red star). These models' starting policies have matching covariances as their experimental counterparts, taken from the first 9 blocks (108 trials) of the target task. We see that only one of the seven models learns to reach the target, while the others' policies diverge during learning. Learning a Gaussian policy distribution over 10,000 time steps with batch size 500 and learning rate $1\times 10^{-4}$.\relax }}{184}{figure.caption.87}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces (a) Agents whose policies converged to the target. (b) Agents whose polices did not converge to the target. (Top rows) Agents' starting policies are spherical Gaussians. (Bottom rows) Agents' starting policies are Gaussians with matching covariances to the sample covariances of the first group (9 blocks of 12 trials) of subjects' trials. (Left columns) Average task space variability across learning time steps. (Right columns) Average null space variability across learning time steps.\relax }}{187}{figure.caption.88}%
